This project implements an ESP32-based self-healing IoT node that can detect, classify, and automatically respond to environmental faults using on-device machine learning. The system continuously monitors its surroundings using a smoke sensor and IR sensor, then uses a trained decision tree model to categorize the current state as normal, warning, or fault. Based on the classification, the node triggers appropriate self-healing actions such as controlling an exhaust fan or actuator through a motor driver.
​

The ESP32 serves as the main controller and edge intelligence unit, running both the sensor interfacing logic and the embedded ML inference. A 16×2 LCD is used to display essential system status messages, while a TFT display shows richer diagnostics such as live sensor readings, fault category, and the recovery action taken. This combination makes the node not just reactive but also explainable, helping users understand why a specific action was triggered.
​

The core idea of the project is to bring AI/ML-enabled fault detection directly onto low-cost embedded hardware, reducing dependency on cloud-based analytics and improving response time. By detecting abnormal conditions early and activating predefined self-healing behaviors locally, the system improves reliability, uptime, and safety for small-scale IoT deployments such as labs, homes, and small industrial setups. The repository includes hardware schematics, firmware code, and the ML model workflow so that the design can be reproduced, extended with new models, or integrated into larger self-healing IoT networks.
